[47.457537, "o", "\r\n## Why the obvious repair silently failed\r\n\r\n`python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` is the standard fix, and it **exited 0 while doing nothing**:\r\n\r\n```\r\nRequirement already satisfied: pip in /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages (24.3.1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n`ensurepip` trusts the dist-info version stamp over the filesystem, concluded pip was present and current, and skipped the work. A success code plus an unchanged broken state — the worst kind of result to debug from.\r\n\r\n## The repair\r\n\r\nI installed pip from the wheel Python ships in-tree, bypassing the version check with `--force-reinstall`:\r\n\r\n```\r\nPYTHONPATH=/usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl \\\r\npython3 -m pip install --force-reinstall --no-index --no-deps \\\r\n  /usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl\r\n```\r\n\r\nUsing the bundled wheel matters: it's local, so no network, no index, and the version exactly matches what the stale metadata claimed — leaving your system consistent rather than silently "]
[47.457574, "o", "upgraded.\r\n\r\n## Verified\r\n\r\n- `python3 -m pip --version`, `pip --version`, `pip3.13 --version` → all report 24.3.1\r\n- `import pip` succeeds\r\n- A **real end-to-end install**: installed `six`, imported it at 1.17.0, then uninstalled it to leave nothing behind\r\n\r\nI also scanned every `dist-info` in site-packages against its `top_level.txt`. pip was the only casualty — no other package had been partially removed.\r\n\r\n## Worth flagging\r\n\r\nSomething deleted that directory. The recursive deletion of `pip/` while the sibling `pip-24.3.1.dist-info/` was untouched is not a pattern normal pip operations produce — an interrupted upgrade would typically corrupt both. Two plausible causes: an aggressive cleanup (e.g. `rm -rf` of a cache/temp path that resolved inside site-packages) or a `__pycache__`-clearing script that took the package with it.\r\n\r\nThe rmdir timestamps are a clue worth checking — site-packages was modified Oct 31, while the dist-info is stamped Jan 14. If you can recall what you ran then, it would "]
[47.457636, "o", "identify the trigger. In the meantime, prefer virtualenvs for project work; that keeps an accident from being written to the system prefix at all, and `python3 -m venv` now works since it depends on `ensurepip` resolving correctly.\r\n\r\nOne side effect to note: the system reports pip 24.3.1, and pip itself now advises upgrading to 26.2.2. I deliberately left that alone — the version you have matches your Python 3.13.1 build, and upgrading pip here is a separate decision from restoring what was deleted.\r\n\r\n[result] role=implementor model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=13 surface=59338fa2ec97\r\n"]
